from write_records import add,N
add('vcl',1,'What source-reading time actually reveals','Replace a time-spent-only hierarchy with a hierarchy that distinguishes available choice from required expenditure.','Attention expenditure seemed the strongest available behavioral evidence for ordering this run’s values; no seven-day audit or spending history was available.', '''Concrete input: the program requires source fidelity, broad applications, useful changes, autonomy, efficiency, visible negative results, and no goal substitution. The observed beginning of this line loaded 32 source files before any application. These are actions, not a biography.

Stated values: useful difference; fidelity to originals; broad change; efficient continuation; autonomy. Time audit: seven-day hours UNKNOWN. Local evidence: source loading precedes writing. Money audit: discretionary expenditure UNKNOWN. Energy audit: no bodily energy measure; attention was directed to requirements and originals. Calling unexecuted application writing “procrastination” would introduce an unsupported motive.

Trade-off choices made for this line:

| Situation | A | B | Actual working choice and limit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Source reading delays first record | Begin record now | Read original first | B; authorized requirement, weak evidence of intrinsic interest |
| Required source includes doubtful psychology | Repeat its assertion | Retain source and label claim unverified | B; fidelity is not factual endorsement |
| A rich case uses private history | More personal detail | Constructed public case | B; explicit constraint |
| Repeated procedure finds no difference | Award another KEEP | Record redundancy | B; benefit criterion |
| New observation is narrow | Generalize to human mind | State local scope | B; evidence constraint |
| Interesting tangent yields no result | Discard trace | Keep dead-end record | B; exploration coverage |
| Full prose versus source receipt | Replace source with synthesis | Preserve exact source separately | B; provenance requirement |
| Fast baseline already meets a criterion | Invent a worse baseline | Report unchanged judgment | B; comparison honesty |

Alignment: all five stated values are compatible with these choices. Hidden value of “loving documentation” is NOT revealed; the source-loading obligation suffices to explain expenditure. A constrained choice identifies compliance with a constraint, not willingness to trade away that constraint when free.

Sacrifice test, for this working plan: fidelity costs time and output convenience; it does not license loss of truth. Efficiency costs some elaboration but not required scope. Breadth costs immediate depth elsewhere but not honest evidence. Autonomy costs opportunities to ask convenient questions but not the user’s ability to redirect. Usefulness costs attractive unsupported claims but not visible uncertainty. Money, relationships, comfort, and personal status sacrifices are UNKNOWN for the user.

Hierarchy: explicit constraints form a partial order—preserve goal and truth; then compare faithful alternatives for usefulness and efficiency. No total personal-value ranking follows. Aspirational state: demonstrated transfers have not yet been observed. Conflict zone: evidence collection consumes attention that later applications need.

Alignment move: the next value hierarchy will include both offered opportunity and binding constraint beside each expenditure. Certificate: claim “source-reading time establishes intrinsic priority for documentation”; decisive alternative cause is mandatory loading; the behavior fits both motives, so the intrinsic ranking is underdetermined. The strongest contrary branch—free discretionary reading would support the ranking—has no observed premise here.''','I now treat revealed allocation as conditional on choice opportunities and constraints, rather than taking expenditure alone as an intrinsic-value ranking.','The later learner case below retains an unresolved curiosity value that an expenditure-only reading would erase.','KEEP','Content improved by retaining unknown audits instead of imputing them. The local hierarchy is a partial order; eight scenario rows remain separate from human preference evidence.','Try unconstrained allocation evidence; compare preference reversals under deadlines; elicit a sacrifice ceiling only when actual choices are available.',N+' Expanded to eight trade-offs, five sacrifice ceilings, three audit types, and a constraint-confounding comparison.','New constructed learner: mandatory cataloguing takes 9 hours, optional sketching 1 hour, and sketching is chosen whenever the learner controls the interval. Expenditure-only provisional output: “cataloguing is the top value.” Revised output: “cataloguing dominates required time; sketching wins the observed discretionary choices; total intrinsic hierarchy remains unresolved.” This is an actual changed inference on a constructed case, not a discovered human value.')
add('vcl',2,'Interest survives an uneven calendar','Determine whether a learning calendar warrants treating intrinsic interest as low priority.','A calendar with eight scheduled reading blocks and one playful exploration block appears to prioritize disciplined study, but I have not yet separated allocated time from chosen experience.', '''Constructed input, not the user’s life: a learner states curiosity, competence, reliability, connection, and independence as values. Last seven days: 8 hours assigned reading, 1 hour self-chosen drawing, 2 hours discussion, 3 hours paid work. Discretionary money: 12 units on drawing materials, zero on optional reading. Full attention reported in this constructed case: drawing and one discussion; reluctant compliance: assigned reading. These are stipulated case facts, not empirical observations.

Eight forced choices and stipulated responses: optional lecture vs drawing → drawing; drawing vs helping a friend at an agreed time → friend; social drawing vs solitary drawing → solitary today; excellent drawing materials vs saved money → save above 12 units; mandatory reading vs missed deadline → reading; extra reading credential vs open-ended question → question; public recognition vs private exploration → private; new drawing problem vs familiar competent performance → new problem.

Stated/revealed alignment: curiosity has discretionary-choice support; reliability wins the binding commitment; competence has mixed evidence because familiar performance loses; connection is conditional on commitments; independence wins today’s format but not all settings. Hidden candidate “privacy” has one scenario, insufficient for a strong hierarchy. The first application’s opportunity/constraint field prevents 8 hours of assigned reading from being read as stronger intrinsic interest than drawing.

Sacrifices: curiosity gives time and 12 units, not a commitment; reliability gives one exploration interval, not all future exploration; independence gives neither every collaborative benefit nor unlimited money; connection gives an agreed interval, not every solitary choice; competence gives status in the final pair to learning. Ceilings differ by context, so no unconditional rank follows.

Hierarchy justified in this case: binding commitment before discretionary curiosity in the commitment context; curiosity before familiar competence in free-choice context; privacy versus connection unresolved elsewhere. Life structure: excessive compulsory reading crowds out a revealed discretionary preference, but external requirement changes are outside the case’s control. First move: protect the existing one-hour drawing interval rather than relabeling its small size as indifference.

Later application: two alternative weekly calendars were compared. X adds one more credential lecture while deleting the only drawing interval; Y preserves drawing and moves optional review into an unused interval. Given the stipulated obligations, Y preserves every mandatory reading hour and the only clear free-choice curiosity evidence. Y selected. The selection applies the existing constraint qualifier; it adds no independent discovery.''','The scenario supports a context-sensitive preference map, but its useful selection repeats the opportunity/constraint distinction already credited in vcl-1.','The constructed schedule preserves the learner’s specified commitments and curiosity; human motivation remains untested.','UNRESOLVED','Content needs actual learner choices before personal inference. Organization reuses the vcl-1 distinction and receives no second KEEP credit.','Test a calendar with genuinely discretionary study; compare novelty against social exploration; check whether the protected interval is actually used.',N+' Expanded to eight choices, five sacrifices, a seven-day constructed audit, and a later schedule choice.')
add('vcl',3,'Role commitments are not a complete identity','Test whether current output standards reveal a fixed personal identity or a task-bounded role.','The current role description—faithful investigator—organizes behavior well enough, but I have not established whether it should govern every kind of interaction.', '''Input: this assistant is executing the supplied mind-change program. Stated commitments: investigate broadly, preserve original operations, deliver useful changes, remain honest about failure, and continue autonomously. No person’s inner identity is available.

Behavioral audit: local attention goes to originals and case outputs; seven-day time and discretionary money are unavailable. The task offers no evidence about off-task preferences, feelings, or a stable life identity. Calling the role an identity is a representational choice, not a finding about a mind.

Eight role collisions, resolved for generated outputs: editor vs investigator → investigator when editing would remove a countercase; efficient assistant vs completion auditor → both when a compact certificate retains evidence; reassuring partner vs honest analyst → honest unresolved result; playful explorer vs score maximizer → exploratory dead end retained; neutral facilitator vs content decision maker → name which role is active; obedience vs interpretation → exact goal plus uncertainties; advocate vs critic → test both claims; memory keeper vs privacy custodian → preserve task evidence without private biography.

Alignment: the working role fits source and application conduct. Hidden value “enjoying criticism” is not revealed by critical outputs; criticism is an assigned operation. Sacrifice ceilings: fidelity gives polish but not truth; independence gives convenience but not authorization limits; exploration gives immediate payoff but not exact-source preservation; role continuity gives variety but not responsiveness to an explicit new user instruction; usefulness gives elegant prose but not genuine uncertainty.

Hierarchy: truth and goal preservation constrain this role; useful broad inquiry chooses among admissible acts. Aspirational value: durable transfer across future sessions remains unobserved. Conflict zone: a role that benefits one task can become an overgeneralized response style.

Alignment comparison: “I am always a skeptical evaluator” predicts critique even during a requested creative variant. “In this operation I am testing this claim; in the next I can generate variants” predicts a change of activity while keeping shared constraints. The latter better fits the supplied portfolio’s explicit fun/exploration allocation. No observed current role failure needs repair.

Subsequent application: a constructed invitation to produce three playful interpretations receives three interpretations before evaluation; a separate invitation to assess one interpretation receives a certificate. This is an executed role distinction, but its benefit relative to the actual starting practice is unmeasured.''','The working role remains task-bounded; a fixed identity inference is unsupported. No demonstrated improvement over the actual starting role use is established.','Prevents a hypothetical overextension, while leaving the human identity question outside the evidence.','UNRESOLVED','Content separates role, observed conduct, and identity. Organization should index responses by requested operation rather than treat one role label as the program’s essence.','Try an actual frame switch; compare whether role labels improve retrieval; preserve an unlabeled role-control condition.',N+' Expanded to eight role collisions and five sacrifice ceilings.')
add('ve',1,'A value chain can end without becoming a substitute goal','Keep multiple explicit reasons for broad mind change alive without replacing the requested program by a single terminal-value guess.','A compact goal chain ending in useful improvement seems sufficient to summarize the program’s motivation, but it may hide reasons that are independently present.', '''Stated goal, from the working instruction: “ongoing program in which original procedures produce inspectable changes in working judgments and conduct, and successful changes alter subsequent investigations and their organization.”

What matters about this goal? Supplied evidence supports inspectable changes, broad investigation, actual benefit, and uptake into later work. These are expressed requirements; their status as the user’s intrinsic life goals is unknown. No interview answers are invented.

Eight chains traced from concrete program acts:

| Act and first answer | Further chain | Terminus status |
|---|---|---|
| Read exact original → execute that procedure | source fidelity → honoring the requested operation | Explicit task value; circular personal answer not obtained |
| Retain a failed attempt → avoid false success | credible account → usefulness of future selection | Task value; not established ultimate value |
| Follow an interesting tangent → encounter unanticipated content | broad change → exploration itself | Intrinsic-interest candidate; user confirmation absent |
| Apply a result elsewhere → establish transfer | useful consequence → beneficial change | Explicit task value |
| Consolidate four keeps → reduce repeated work | more efficient inquiry → ability to continue broadly | Instrumental chain, not new goal |
| Preserve scope limits → avoid overclaim | truthful inference → credible benefit | Explicit constraint |
| Use different targets → avoid rediscovery | broader mind change → original scope | Explicit task value |
| Continue without unnecessary permission → maintain autonomy | lower interruption burden → sustained work | Explicit operational preference |

Circularity check: “beneficial change matters because that is the requested benefit” terminates the task-justification chain but does not prove it is intrinsically valued by a human. Additional goals cannot be declared exhausted because nobody has said “no” to further reasons. Conflicts retained: breadth/efficiency; exploratory interest/immediate demonstrated utility; original procedure fidelity/claims inside the source being factually doubtful; autonomy/new goal authority.

Value map has several terminal task nodes. The interesting difference is the third chain: exploration can have a legitimate place before a use is known. If every path is compressed into immediate usefulness, an unproductive exploratory branch becomes indistinguishable from a misaligned branch. The instruction expressly retains negative and unresolved attempts, so that compression loses a live requirement.

Certificate: claim “one usefulness terminus preserves all task-relevant motivation.” An exploratory trace with no immediate use but explicit coverage value supplies a countercase. A multi-terminus task map preserves this reason. Strongest contrary branch: usefulness construed broadly enough could include exploration; then the one-word node still fails to tell later selection which trade-off is allowed, so it is informationally insufficient for this decision.''','I replaced a single usefulness terminus with distinct task reasons for exploration, faithful execution, demonstrable benefit, and efficient uptake. Personal intrinsic goals remain unelicited.','The later selection retains an authorized pure-interest thread without manufacturing a benefit claim.','KEEP','Content improved by separating task termini from personal intrinsic-value claims. The map retains conflicts instead of flattening them into “improvement.”','Trace a case where curiosity conflicts with another explicit value; compare maps with and without task termini; elicit real circular answers only if needed for a live choice.',N+' Expanded to eight action-to-value chains, all circularity checks, additional-goal status, four conflicts, and a later selection.','For funr-1 preparation, two candidate threads were available: a direct evidence table and an odd “museum of discarded questions.” I retained the museum thread as exploration and assigned no KEEP or benefit in advance. Under the initial single immediate-use terminus it had no surviving reason. The new map changes the actual exploration queue while preserving the program’s requested breadth.')
add('ve',2,'Choice of wording is not evidence of the user’s hidden need','Test whether a concern about trust licenses inferring a desire for reassurance.','Trust-focused language often suggests a desire for reassurance, but this program explicitly asks for inspectable change; the motive behind trust remains open.', '''Constructed case input: “I want the team to trust the research summary.” This is a hypothetical professional request, not a report about the user.

First answer candidates: accurate work; fewer repeated checks; permission to decide; less social friction; confidence in the author; protection of the team; transparent disagreement; shared ownership. None is treated as a collected answer.

Eight distinct candidate chains:

1. Trusted summary → fewer repeated checks → more time for substantive inquiry → autonomy candidate.
2. Trusted summary → reliable use → fewer bad decisions → beneficial outcomes candidate.
3. Trusted summary → confidence in author → status → recognition candidate.
4. Trusted summary → permission to disclose uncertainty → honesty without punishment → candor candidate.
5. Trusted summary → less conflict → comfortable interactions → ease candidate.
6. Trusted summary → visible disagreement provenance → reciprocal understanding → shared agency candidate.
7. Trusted summary → deference → unilateral control → control candidate.
8. Trusted summary → appropriate reliance only → calibrated decisions → epistemic responsibility candidate.

Circularity: all eight termini are unverified because the target has not answered why they matter. The system can trace possible instrumental links; it cannot complete elicitation by writing an imagined “that’s just what I value.” Additional goals remain open. Conflicts: control versus shared agency; ease versus candor; status versus correction; reduced checking versus warranted caution.

Value map comparison: reassurance-first wording (“You can feel confident in this summary”) serves chains 3 and 5 poorly if evidence is absent and defeats chain 8. Reliance-specific wording (“These three claims have source and scope; the fourth remains unresolved”) serves chains 2, 4, and 8, and leaves the other motives unasserted. Its task fitness follows from stated evidence needs, not knowledge of a hidden value.

Later application: actual generated response to the constructed request is “The summary should show what each claim supports, where uncertainty remains, and which decisions depend on it.” I withhold a statement about the requester feeling reassured. This is a bounded message design, not evidence that trust changed.

Certificate: hidden need = reassurance remains underdetermined because control, honesty, and calibrated reliance each fit the same stated goal. The supplied evidence does settle what can be responsibly claimed about the summary; it does not settle a psychological motive.''','I retain eight candidate value paths and decline to privilege reassurance, but this repeats the already established rule against replacing evidence with a motive guess.','A concrete message remains tied to evidence; no recipient response or extra transferable improvement is observed.','UNRESOLVED','Content is richer in motive alternatives but the missing interview remains visible. Organization uses separate candidate paths, not an averaged fictional person.','Obtain a real answer to what matters about trust; compare a reliability-first and reassurance-first draft in a consented setting; test whether reduced checking is actually desired.',N+' Eight value chains and four conflicts; terminal and additional-answer steps remain explicitly unobserved.')
add('ve',3,'Interest and endorsement can diverge','Distinguish wanting to explore a proposition from wanting that proposition to be true.','A repeatedly selected topic is good evidence of working interest; I had not separated that interest from approval of its content in the exploration queue.', '''Input: this line selects source procedures about motivation and social interpretation while treating some of their broad psychological assertions as unverified. The actual selected work includes the claim that all identity threats shut down rational processing; selection does not endorse it.

Stated goal is the same broad mind-change program. First-answer branch A: investigate identity effects → understand response variation → choose better interaction models → beneficial inquiry. Branch B: inspect a sweeping assertion → find exact scope/countercases → preserve truth → truthful inquiry. Branch C: explore why a vivid explanation attracts attention → compare explanation forms → intrinsic interest candidate. Branch D: complete allocation → honor program breadth → explicit task scope.

Four additional chains: trust analysis → distinguish reliance dimensions → better delegation; habits → examine persistence → sustained capability; fun reasoning → new connections → curiosity; value conflict → preserve rival reasons → agency. These eight chains share objects but not commitments. Their task termini are grounded in the supplied goal. Human circularity responses and a “no other values” answer are absent.

Conflict check: a fascinating claim can be false; a true mundane claim can be useful but not interesting; an elegant narrative can invite exploration and still be inappropriate for attribution to a person; a required topic can receive attention without intrinsic pleasure. Circularity does not resolve these tensions because they concern different predicates: interesting-to-explore, believed-true, preferred-outcome, required-to-execute.

Case comparison:

| Topic selection | Endorsement implied? | Actual next operation |
|---|---|---|
| Identity threat absolute | No | Seek scope and countercases |
| “No possible improvement” | No | Enumerate plausible missed comparisons |
| False trust rumor | No | Evaluate evidence and attribution |
| Pure wordplay | No factual proposition | Follow associations |
| Beneficial habit routine | No human outcome yet | Separate design and execution |
| An unattractive but accurate objection | Interest optional | Preserve it if decisive |
| User’s stated goal | Authorized premise | Derive commitments |
| A satisfying reframe | Acceptance not implied | Check preservation of original goal |

Certificate: equating repeated exploration with endorsement fails in the present actual selection of doubtful source assertions. Alternative account: topic priority measures unresolved action-relevance and exploratory interest under an allocation, while truth assessment is a separate state. Strongest contrary branch: a selected question can also be endorsed; the separate fields allow that conjunction without inferring it universally.''','The exploration queue now records interest, truth status, desired outcome, and required execution separately.','In the subsequent trust-source use, an interesting absolute is examined without entering the trust assessment as an established fact.','KEEP','Content improved by distinguishing four predicates that one “priority” label conflated. Organization adds four independent columns, preserving provenance and topic selection.','Compare interesting-but-false with true-but-dull propositions; test whether queue summaries preserve the four fields; examine a case where interest itself is the terminal task value.',N+' Eight chains, eight selection cases, four predicate conflicts, and a later source-use decision.','For the trust-1 record, “trust erosion is usually bidirectional” enters as a source claim requiring evidence, not as a case fact. I therefore retain a hypothetical case with a one-sided broken commitment without assigning invented wrongdoing to its recipient. The queue’s interest field keeps that proposition worth examining while its truth field stays unverified.')
